  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 106 PM MDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nAn unusually warm October weather pattern remains firmly \nestablished across eastern Idaho today. Afternoon satellite \nimagery displays mid-to-high level cloudiness drifting overhead,\nbut near-record to record high temperatures are still expected \nas lower-elevation valleys climb into the lower-to-mid 80s. \nHigh-resolution convective-allowing models (CAMs) indicate a \nlow-end chance for an isolated shower or thunderstorm to develop\nlater this afternoon across the South Hills or along the \nMontana Divide. While atmospheric moisture is limited\u2014keeping \nprecipitation accumulation minimal\u2014passing cells will be capable\nof generating localized gusty downdraft winds. The vast \nmajority of eastern Idaho will remain dry and unseasonably hot.\n\nThe upper-level ridge anchored to our south gets suppressed on \nTuesday as 500 mb heights dip slightly across the Intermountain \nWest. In response, daytime maximum temperatures will drop a few \ndegrees Tuesday through Friday under a predominantly zonal flow \nregime. Despite this minor temperature reduction, afternoon \nhighs will continue to run 10 to 15 degrees above seasonal \nclimatological norms. Dry conditions and light-to-moderate \nsurface winds will prevail through the end of the workweek.\n\nWeather conditions begin to transition on Saturday as global \nguidance tracks a potent upper-level low digging into the \nPacific Northwest. As the regional pressure gradient tightens \nahead of the approaching trough, surface winds will increase \nmarkedly across the Snake Plain and higher terrain. Increased \ncloud shading and wind mixing will cap Saturday high \ntemperatures in the 60s and 70s. While these readings remain \nslightly above seasonal averages, Saturday marks the leading \nedge of a significant airmass change.\n\nConfidence continues to increase in a drastic cooldown arriving\nSunday and extending into early next week as the parent upper\nlow crosses the region. While ensemble guidance exhibits ongoing\nspread regarding the exact depth and track of the system,\nsignificant cold advection, widespread precipitation, and\nelevated winds appear increasingly likely. Current National\nBlend of Models (NBM) thermal guidance appears to sit on the\nwarm side of latest model runs, suggesting daily high\ntemperatures may trend even lower in upcoming forecast cycles.\nHigh-elevation mountain snow is looking increasingly probable\nSunday night into Monday, with guidance indicating a non-zero\npossibility for valley snow flakes depending on how rapidly cold\nair fills in behind the front. Additionally, valley frost/freeze\nconditions appear possible for early next week. Forecast details\nwill be refined over the coming days as guidance converges, but\na definitive shift toward true fall weather is on the horizon.",
